{
  "points": [
    {
      "point": "Surplus Property Sale Approval: The King County Council's Budget and Fiscal Management Committee reviewed the sale of surplus property at Fauntleroy Way No. 5, which was declared surplus on September 8th, 2021 (per FMD analysis). The committee approved Ordinance 2026-0108 to authorize this sale based on a $600,000 purchase price determined by the sales comparison approach among five land transactions. This approval also advanced the proposal for the 39.1 acres in Townsville District 9 (via Proposed Ordinance 2026-0108), which is intended to fund the acquisition of new open space and improvements to existing recreation facilities.",
      "speakers": [
        "Rod Domowsk",
        "Amanda Tran"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    },
    {
      "point": "Ordinance 2026-0108: Townsville District 9 Property Sale. This ordinance outlines the sale of 39.1 acres of agricultural land, previously owned by King County Water and Land Resources Division (Willard). The purchase price was set at $600,000 based on an appraisal using a sales comparison approach to ensure equitable valuation while incorporating a reserved agricultural easement into the bargain and sale deed.",
      "speakers": [
        "Amanda Tran",
        "Rod Domowsk"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    },
    {
      "point": "Agricultural Land Preservation: The property is subject to a restrictive covenant requiring preservation as agricultural land for thirty years. This duration was imposed by the Washington State Department of Natural Resources (DNR) in 2001 and reflects the condition set during the acquisition of trust land, though the exact reason for not setting perpetuity was questioned.",
      "speakers": [
        "Rod Domowsk",
        "Amanda Tran"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    },
    {
      "point": "Ordinance 2026-0069: Clark Lake Park Property Sale. This ordinance addresses the sale of approximately 180 square feet of county property in Kent, Washington (Council District 5), for roughly $147,000. The historical context reveals that King County acquired this land with state funding requiring permanent preservation; however, a prior Interlocal Agreement (ILA) led to unresolved obligations (payment/transfer of replacement park land). This ordinance aims to resolve the obligation and preserve open space through a restrictive covenant.",
      "speakers": [
        "yoyoyo",
        "Rod Domowsk"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    },
    {
      "point": "Ordinance Approval and Timing: Due to expiring legal deadlines, the committee is urged to approve Proposed Ordinance 2026-0108 today. This approval allows for a motion (Amendment One) that extends the contingency/approval period from June 17th to August 17th, 2026. This timing ensures the underlying ordinance remains effective and resolves outstanding obligations related to the sale of land in Clark Lake Park.",
      "speakers": [
        "Rod Domowsk",
        "yoyoyo"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    }
  ]
}

