{
  "points": [
    {
      "point": "The Executive report on Aurora Oaks Operations details a complex situation involving an $8.1 million loan and the sustainability of C H S's interim loan program for supporting the Aurora Oaks Enhanced Shelter. Operating at 60-bed capacity, Lake City Partners Ending Homelessness (LCP) manages this shelter under agreement with C H S; however, due to federal funding volatility and limited state/local money, C H S notified staff of its inability to fulfill loan obligations as maturity approached. Key discussion points include: a) Addressing the 2026-2027 Biennial Budget Ordinance's Proviso three restriction on funds for continued shelter operation; b) Analyzing options (amending King County Code/loan extension, foreclosure by DC HS, or purchase of property); c) Determining financial feasibility based on required partial redevelopment and public funding forecasts. The Executive recommends amending the loan program to allow an extension while acknowledging it's not a permanent fix. Two amendments are presented: Amendment S1 for technical changes and Amendment One to S1, which allows waiving interest charges and increasing the necessary bed threshold from 50 to 60 to accommodate the ending project's needs.",
      "speakers": [
        "Rod Domowski",
        "Executive staff",
        "C H S representatives"
      ],
